    "global index": "MathQA_436",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: a 12 meter long wire is cut into two pieces . if the longer piece is then used to form a perimeter of a square , what is the probability that the area of the square will be more than 4 if the original wire was cut at an arbitrary point ?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1 / 6\nB. 2 / 3\nC. 3 / 10\nD. 1 / 3\nE. 2 / 5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let the cut point be at distance $x$ from one end, where $0 < x < 12$. The longer piece has length $\\max(x, 12 - x)$, which is $> 6$ (since the longer piece must exceed half the wire). For the square formed from the longer piece, side length $s = \\frac{\\max(x, 12 - x)}{4}$, so area $A = s^2 = \\left(\\frac{\\max(x, 12 - x)}{4}\\right)^2$. We want $A > 4$, i.e., $\\left(\\frac{\\max(x, 12 - x)}{4}\\right)^2 > 4 \\Rightarrow \\max(x, 12 - x) > 8$. Since $\\max(x, 12 - x) > 8$ when $x < 4$ or $x > 8$, and $x$ is uniformly distributed over $(0,12)$, the favorable length is $4 + 4 = 8$, so probability is $8/12 = 2/3$.  \nNote: Although the longer piece must be $>6$, the condition $\\max(x,12-x)>8$ already ensures it is the longer piece (since $8>6$), and the uniform cut assumption covers all points equally.  \nThus, the probability is $2/3$.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 186,
        "output_tokens": 307,
        "total_tokens": 493
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
