      "generated_answer": "We are given the recurrence: $ y[n] - 3y[n-1] + 2y[n-2] = 0 $ for $ n \\geq 2 $, with $ y[0] = 2 $, $ y[1] = 3 $.  \nComputing $ y[2] = 3y[1] - 2y[0] = 3(3) - 2(2) = 9 - 4 = 5 $, and $ y[3] = 3y[2] - 2y[1] = 3(5) - 2(3) = 15 - 6 = 9 $.  \nThus, the sequence starting at $ n=2 $ is $ y[2]=5, y[3]=9, \\dots $, matching option C: $ y[n] = 2, 5, 9, \\dots $ (though it includes $ y[0] $, the pattern from $ n=2 $ is correct).  \nThe correct answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
