    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10298",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 6-pole 3-phase 60-cycle alternator has 12 slots per pole and four conductors per slot. The winding is five-sixths pitch. There are 2,500,000 maxwells (= 0.025 weber) entering the armature from each north pole, and this flux is sinusoidally distributed along the air gap. The armature coils are all connected in series. The winding is Y-connected. Determine the open-circuit emf of the alternator.\n\nOptions: \nA. 296 volts\nB. 480 volts\nC. 425 volts\nD. 350 volts\nE. 550 volts\nF. 600 volts\nG. 513 volts\nH. 250 volts\nI. 275 volts\nJ. 700 volts\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "First, calculate the number of slots per pole per phase: 12 slots/pole ÷ 3 phases = 4 slots/pole/phase. Total slots = 6 poles × 12 = 72 slots. Conductors per phase = (72 slots × 4 conductors/slot) ÷ 3 = 96 conductors. Turns per phase = 96 ÷ 2 = 48 turns. Flux per pole = 0.025 Wb. Frequency = 60 Hz. Pitch factor for 5/6 pitch: kₚ = sin(β/2) where β = (1 – 5/6)×180° = 30°, so kₚ = sin(15°) ≈ 0.2588? Wait—correct formula: kₚ = sin(π·y/τ) = sin(5π/6) = sin(150°) = 0.5? Actually, pitch factor kₚ = sin(α/2) where α is the coil span in electrical degrees; full pitch = 180°, so 5/6 pitch = 150°, so kₚ = sin(150°/2) = sin(75°) ≈ 0.9659. Distribution factor: m = 4 slots/phase/pole, β = 180°/12 = 15° per slot, so k_d = sin(mβ/2)/[m·sin(β/2)] = sin(4×15°/2)/[4·sin(7.5°)] = sin(30°)/[4·sin(7.5°)] = 0.5/[4×0.1305] ≈ 0.5/0.522 ≈ 0.958. EMF per phase = 4.44 × f × N_ph × Φ × kₚ × k_d = 4.44 × 60 × 48 × 0.025 × 0.9659 × 0.958 ≈ 4.44×60=266.4; ×48=12,787.2; ×0.025=319.68; ×0.9659≈308.7; ×0.958≈295.8 V. Line voltage for Y-connection = √3 × V_ph ≈ 1.732 × 295.8 ≈ 512.5 V ≈ 513 V.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
