      "generated_answer": "The best evidence for an association between sugar intake and dental caries risk comes from prospective cohort studies, which follow individuals over time to assess exposure (sugar consumption) and outcome (caries development), minimizing recall bias and establishing temporal sequence. While randomized controlled trials (RCTs) are strongest for causation, ethical and practical constraints make long-term RCTs of sugar intake in caries prevention difficult; thus, high-quality cohort studies (e.g., the famous Dunedin Study) provide the most robust observational evidence. Meta-analyses of such cohort studies (e.g., WHO and CFAS reviews) further strengthen conclusions, but the foundational evidence derives from cohort designs.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
