    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Object permanence—the understanding that objects continue to exist even when out of sight—is a key milestone in Piaget’s theory and emerges during the sensorimotor stage, specifically around 8–12 months of age. This stage (birth to about 2 years) is characterized by learning through sensory experiences and motor actions, with object permanence marking a major cognitive shift from reflexive to intentional behavior. Before this stage, infants behave as if objects cease to exist once hidden, but once object permanence develops, they actively search for hidden objects.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
