or? C major: C–D–E–F–G–A–B. ♭6 is A♭, not B.\n\nB is ♯5 of G minor? Not relevant.\n\nActually, **B is the root of an Italian sixth chord in E major?** No—♭6 of E is C.\n\nWait—**E major: ♭6 = C**, so Italian sixth = C–E–G♯. Not helpful.\n\nLet’s try **D major**: ♭6 = B♭. Italian sixth = B♭–D–F.\n\nBut **option C: G–B–♭E** = G–B–D♯. In **E major**, G is ♭3, B is 5th, D♯ is ♯5—no.\n\nIn **C major**, G is 5th, B is ♯5, D♯ = ♯5 again.\n\nWait—**G–B–D♯** is a G augmented triad (G–B–D♯). Augmented triads are *components* of augmented sixth chords but not themselves augmented sixth chords.\n\nHowever, the **German sixth chord** in E major is C–E–G–B♭. Not matching.\n\nGiven all this confusion, and noting that **option C: G–B–♭E** spells G–B–D♯ (since ♭E = D♯), and in **E♭ major**, G is the ♭6, and G–B–D♯ is the *Italian sixth* chord (root G, major third B, augmented fifth D♯). So if the question meant \"B as the *third* of the chord\", then C fits. But the question says \"with B as the root\", so none fit perfectly.\n\nBut wait—in **B major**, the ♭6 is G♮. So the Italian sixth chord in B major is G–B–D♯—exactly option **C** (since ♭E = D♯). Yes! In B major, scale: B–C♯–D♯–E♯–F♯–G♯–A♯. ♭6 = G♮. So the Italian sixth chord is built on G♮: G–B–D♯ (root G, major third B, augmented fifth D♯). So **G is the root**, B is the *third*, D♯ is the *augmented fifth*. But the question says *\"with B as the root\"*, so this is inconsistent.\n\nUnless the question has a typo and means \"B as the *third*\". Given that, option C is the only one that forms a valid Italian sixth chord (G–B–D♯), and B is a chord tone.\n\nAlternatively, **option A: 𝄪F-B-♯D** = F𝄪–B–D♯ = G–B–D♯ (same as C). So A and C are enharmonically identical: A is F𝄪–B–D♯ = G–B–D♯, and C is G–B–♭E = G–B–D♯. So both A and C spell the same chord. But A uses F𝄪 (enharmonic G), and C uses G directly. Since the question asks for the *correct* spelling, and F𝄪 is a valid (though unusual) spelling for G, but in standard notation,