    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4645",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"We found that not only was it a civil war, an effort by a people who had for years been seeking their liberation from any colonial influence whatsoever, but also we found that the Vietnamese whom we had enthusiastically molded after our own image were hard put to take up the fight against the threat we were supposedly saving them from.\n\"We found most people didn't even know the difference between communism and democracy. They only wanted to work in rice paddies without helicopters strafing them and bombs with napalm burning their villages and tearing their country apart. They wanted everything to do with the war, particularly with this foreign presence of the United States of America, to leave them alone in peace, and they practiced the art of survival by siding with whichever military force was present at a particular time, be it Viet Cong, North Vietnamese or American.\"\nJohn Kerry, 1971\nThe most important factor in the defeat of Democratic presidential candidates in the elections of 1952 and 1968 was\n\nOptions: \nA. the candidates' lack of military service records\nB. the rise of third-party candidates that split the Democratic vote\nC. the American public's desire to avoid conflict and return to a more conservative political and social life\nD. the Democratic Party's perceived weakness on national security issues\nE. the Democratic candidates' controversial positions on civil rights legislation\nF. the Democratic candidates' inability to effectively communicate their policies to the public\nG. the Democratic Party's unequivocal support of the Equal Rights Amendment\nH. the Democratic Party platform pledge to increase taxes in order to pay off the national debt\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The passage by John Kerry highlights the disconnect between U.S. policy and Vietnamese popular sentiment, emphasizing war fatigue and disillusionment with foreign intervention—context that aligns with the broader national mood in the U.S. during the late 1960s. In both 1952 and 1968, the American public expressed a strong desire to end conflict (Korean War in ’52, Vietnam War in ’68) and return to stability, favoring candidates who promised peace and order—Eisenhower in ’52 and Nixon in ’68—while the Democratic candidates (Stevenson and Humphrey) were perceived as tied to prolonged, unpopular wars. Although civil rights and national security were factors, the dominant theme across both elections was the public’s yearning to avoid further conflict and embrace a more conservative, peace-oriented direction.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 423,
        "output_tokens": 183,
        "total_tokens": 606
      },
