      "generated_answer": "The first passage by Bentinck shows a European official reluctantly banning sati not for economic gain or cultural imposition, but out of moral concern—even while acknowledging political risks—thus challenging the assumption that Western colonizers were uniformly eager to force their cultural norms. This nuanced stance contradicts option C, which portrays imperialism as uniformly culturally aggressive. The passage reveals internal moral conflict, undermining the stereotype of consistent, unthinking cultural imposition.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
