    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The passage by John Kerry emphasizes the Vietnamese people’s desire to be left alone from U.S. military involvement and highlights the disconnect between U.S. ideological framing and local realities—clearly pointing to opposition to U.S. involvement in Vietnam as a central concern. The Counterculture Movement of the 1960s was deeply intertwined with anti-war sentiment, especially against the Vietnam War, and also strongly supported civil rights, including racial equality and social justice. While other issues like the draft, environmentalism, and women’s rights were important, the movement’s two most defining and interconnected political issues were U.S. involvement in Vietnam and the civil rights movement.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
