    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Populist Party’s success was most directly fueled by growing discontent among rural farmers and laborers over the concentration of corporate and banking power, which they believed exploited ordinary citizens—this is clearly reflected in the 1892 platform’s condemnation of trusts, banks, and demonetization of silver. While the expansion of railroads (F) and economic hardship (B) played roles, option H most directly captures the ideological catalyst: resistance to corporate and financial elites. The Populists arose not from labor unions in the North or prohibition, but from agrarian outrage over economic inequality.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
